## Chapter 6 Summary

The measurement of the nuclear modification factor of non-photonic electrons that originate from heavy quarks decays has shown that it exhibits a suppression for the large values of transverse momentum, which is similar to the one measured for the light flavor hadrons. This contradicts the theoretical prediction, thus in this study an attempt was made to separate the beauty and charm contributions in the non-photonic electron spectrum, by measuring the charm meson production in the function of azimuthal angular correlations between the \(\mathrm{D}^{0}\) or \(\mathrm{D}^{*}\) and the electron that presumably comes form the heavy flavor semileptonic decay.

The results shows that the capabilities of the current setup of the STAR detector do not allow to perform this kind of measurement in the Gold-Gold collisions at 200 GeV, as the system does not provide the resolution necessary to separate the charm decays from the combinatorial background. Starting form 2014 STAR will be equipped with a new vertex detector, Heavy Flavor Tracker, which is designed to improve the resolution to 30 \(\mu\)m which will allow to identify the origin of non-photonic electrons as well as reconstruct the heavy flavor meson decay vertices.

The outcome from the \(\mathrm{e-D}^{*}\) angular correlations in p+p collisions at 500 GeV has allowed to estimate, for the first time with this method, the contribution of beauty to the total open heavy flavor production in this system. The obtained value is within the errors in agreement with the beauty to open heavy flavor ratio measured for the p+p collisions at 200 GeV. Furthermore it extends the measurement of beauty to open heavy flavor from the electron-hadron correlations to the lower \(\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{T}}\).

The inclusive spectra of the charm particles (D0 and D*) has been measured by STAR in proton-proton minimum bias collisions in the range of \(\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{T}}\leq 7\mathrm{GeV/c}\). The new study of D* performed on the high tower dataset allowed to extend the spectra up to \(\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{T}}=18\) GeV/c for the proton-proton system at 500 GeV. That range of the pT has never been reached before for D* meson analysis on any system measured in STAR.

